  content: `
  <p>Planning your first trip to Europe sounds exciting until you open a map.</p>

  <p>Paris is there. Rome is there. Barcelona, Amsterdam, Prague, Vienna, London, Athens...</p>

  <p>Suddenly, your ten-day holiday has somehow become a plan to visit eight countries.</p>

  <p>Europe makes it easy to forget how much time you actually need to experience a place.</p>

  <p>So how many days are enough for a first trip?</p>

  <p>There is no single perfect number, but for most travelers, <strong>7 to 14 days</strong> is enough to experience a small part of Europe properly without turning the entire trip into a transportation marathon.</p>

  <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1499856871958-5b9627545d1a?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1600&q=80" alt="European city street with historic architecture" class="in-article-img" />

  <blockquote class="editorial-quote">
    "Your first trip to Europe should leave you wanting to come back, not wondering when you last slept."
  </blockquote>

  <h2>🇪🇺 First, Accept That You Can't See All of Europe</h2>

  <p>This is the hardest lesson for first-time visitors.</p>

  <p>Europe may look relatively small on a map, but the experience of moving between countries is very different from simply looking at the distance between them.</p>

  <p>Every change of city means packing, checking out, traveling to a station or airport, waiting, traveling again, and finding your way around a new place.</p>

  <p>Do that every day and a large part of your holiday disappears in transit.</p>

  <p>Instead of asking, "How many countries can I visit?", ask:</p>

  <p><strong>"How many places can I actually enjoy?"</strong></p>

  <h2>📅 7 Days — Keep It Simple</h2>

  <p>With one week, resist the temptation to visit five cities.</p>

  <p>Choose one major city and explore it properly, or combine two destinations that are easy to connect.</p>

  <p>For example, you could spend most of your trip in Paris and add a few days somewhere nearby, depending on your interests and transport options.</p>

  <p>Another option is to choose a compact route such as Prague and Vienna, giving yourself enough time in both rather than trying to add several more stops.</p>

  <div class="destination-card">
    <h3>🗓️ A Comfortable 7-Day Structure</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Arrive and settle in.</li>
      <li><strong>Days 2–4:</strong> Explore your main city.</li>
      <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Travel to a second destination.</li>
      <li><strong>Days 6–7:</strong> Explore and enjoy the second destination.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>The exact cities can change, but the principle stays the same: <strong>fewer stops, more time.</strong></p>

  <h2>🏛️ 10 Days — The Sweet Spot for Many Travelers</h2>

  <p>Ten days gives you noticeably more freedom.</p>

  <p>You can comfortably build a trip around two or three cities, particularly if they are well connected.</p>

  <p>For example, a first-time itinerary could combine cities such as Paris, Amsterdam, and Brussels, or Rome, Florence, and Venice.</p>

  <p>The key is to avoid adding another destination simply because it is "only a few hours away."</p>

  <p>Those few hours add up once you include hotel checkouts, stations, airports, waiting, and getting settled again.</p>

  <p>Give each destination enough time to develop its own rhythm.</p>

  <h2>🌍 14 Days — Enough for a Real Multi-City Trip</h2>

  <p>Two weeks is where a first Europe trip starts to feel much more flexible.</p>

  <p>You can explore three or four destinations without necessarily rushing every day.</p>

  <p>You could create a route through several neighboring countries or focus on one region.</p>

  <p>For example, a traveler interested in Central Europe could build a route around Prague, Vienna, Budapest, and another nearby destination.</p>

  <p>Someone interested in southern Europe could focus on Italy, Spain, Portugal, or a combination of nearby cities.</p>

  <p>The important part is keeping the route logical.</p>

  <h2>🗺️ 21 Days — Slow Down and Go Deeper</h2>

  <p>Three weeks gives you a completely different opportunity.</p>

  <p>You can visit several cities while still leaving room for slower days, day trips, nature, smaller towns, and spontaneous discoveries.</p>

  <p>You also have more flexibility if weather changes or you decide you love one destination and want to stay longer.</p>

  <p>If you have three weeks, consider choosing a region rather than trying to "do Europe."</p>

  <div class="flight-tips-card">
    <h3>✈️ Example Trip Lengths</h3>

    <p><strong>7 days:</strong> 1–2 destinations.</p>

    <p><strong>10 days:</strong> 2–3 destinations.</p>

    <p><strong>14 days:</strong> 3–4 destinations.</p>

    <p><strong>21 days:</strong> 4–6 destinations, depending on the route and pace.</p>

    <p><strong>30+ days:</strong> Consider slow travel and spending longer in each region.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>🚆 Don't Forget Travel Days</h2>

  <p>This is one of the biggest mistakes first-time Europe travelers make.</p>

  <p>If you have ten days, you do not really have ten full sightseeing days if you are changing cities multiple times.</p>

  <p>A travel day can consume half a day or more once everything is included.</p>

  <p>That is why a three-city itinerary can sometimes feel more tiring than it looks on paper.</p>

  <p>When planning, treat major travel days as partial sightseeing days.</p>

  <h2>✈️ Start With Your Flights</h2>

  <p>Your flight schedule can shape your entire itinerary.</p>

  <p>A late arrival may mean that your first day is mostly about checking into your hotel and getting some rest.</p>

  <p>An early departure can make your final day much shorter than expected.</p>

  <p>Compare flight options before locking in your route. <strong>FlySava</strong> can help you compare different flights, airlines, dates, and routes so you can choose an arrival and departure schedule that actually works with your itinerary.</p>

  <p>Then build your European route around those flights rather than trying to force the flights into an already complicated plan.</p>

  <h2>🏨 Don't Change Hotels Every Night</h2>

  <p>There is a hidden cost to moving too often.</p>

  <p>Every hotel change means packing your bags, checking out, traveling, checking in again, and reorganizing everything.</p>

  <p>If you are staying somewhere for only one night, you may spend more time arriving and leaving than experiencing the destination.</p>

  <p>For most first trips, staying at least two or three nights in a city is a much more comfortable approach.</p>

  <h2>☕ Leave Room for the Unexpected</h2>

  <p>Your itinerary should not be a list of obligations.</p>

  <p>Maybe you find a café you love. Maybe a local recommends a neighborhood you had never heard of. Maybe it rains on the day you planned to visit a viewpoint.</p>

  <p>Leave enough empty space to change your plans.</p>

  <p>Some of your favorite European memories may come from the things you never planned to do.</p>

  <blockquote class="editorial-quote">
    "A little empty space in your itinerary is where unexpected travel stories begin."
  </blockquote>

  <h2>💰 Does a Longer Europe Trip Always Cost More?</h2>

  <p>Not necessarily.</p>

  <p>A longer trip naturally means more accommodation and daily expenses, but moving constantly can also become expensive.</p>

  <p>More flights, trains, taxis, airport transfers, and short hotel stays can quickly add up.</p>

  <p>A slower two-week trip can sometimes feel better value than trying to pack the same number of destinations into seven days.</p>

  <div class="budget-card">
    <h3>💰 Ways to Control Your Europe Budget</h3>

    <p><strong>Stay longer:</strong> Avoid paying for constant hotel changes.</p>

    <p><strong>Use trains where practical:</strong> Some European routes connect major cities efficiently.</p>

    <p><strong>Compare flights:</strong> Check different dates and routes before booking.</p>

    <p><strong>Mix paid and free activities:</strong> Balance major attractions with parks, neighborhoods, markets, and walking.</p>

    <p><strong>Travel outside peak periods:</strong> When your schedule allows, consider shoulder seasons.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>🧭 How to Choose Your First European Route</h2>

  <p>Start with your interests rather than a country checklist.</p>

  <p>If you love art and architecture, build around cities that excel at those things.</p>

  <p>If you love food, create a route where eating is part of the experience.</p>

  <p>If you want beaches and warm weather, focus on southern Europe.</p>

  <p>If you want history and charming old cities, Central Europe may be a better fit.</p>

  <p>There is no "correct" first Europe itinerary.</p>

  <p>There is only the itinerary that fits what you actually want to experience.</p>

  <h2>📌 The Biggest First-Timer Mistake</h2>

  <p>Trying to prove that you got your money's worth.</p>

  <p>You do not need to visit six countries because you flew all the way to Europe.</p>

  <p>You do not need a photograph in front of every famous landmark.</p>

  <p>You do not need to wake up at 6 AM every morning.</p>

  <p>A trip is not successful because you checked the most boxes.</p>

  <p>It is successful because you came home with experiences you actually remember.</p>

  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>Is 7 days enough for a first trip to Europe?</summary>
    <p>Yes, if you keep your itinerary focused. Choose one major city or two destinations that are easy to connect rather than trying to cover several countries.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>Is 10 days enough for Europe?</summary>
    <p>Ten days can work very well for a first trip. Two or three well-connected destinations can give you a good mix of sightseeing and relaxed exploration.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>How many European cities should I visit in two weeks?</summary>
    <p>Three or four cities is a reasonable starting point, depending on the distances and transport connections. Fewer cities allow for a slower and more relaxed trip.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>Should I visit multiple countries on my first Europe trip?</summary>
    <p>You can, but you do not have to. A multi-country trip works best when the destinations are geographically close and well connected.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="faq-accordion">
    <summary>What is the best way to travel around Europe?</summary>
    <p>It depends on your route. Trains can be convenient for many city-to-city journeys, while flights may make more sense for longer distances or certain routes. Compare the total journey time and cost before choosing.</p>
  </details>

  <h2>Final Thoughts</h2>

  <p>Your first trip to Europe does not need to cover Europe.</p>

  <p>Pick a region. Choose a few places. Give yourself time to actually experience them.</p>

  <p>If you have one week, keep it simple. With ten days, add another destination. With two or three weeks, slow down and explore deeper.</p>

  <blockquote class="editorial-quote">
    "Europe will still be there when you come back. You don't have to see it all the first time."
  </blockquote>

  <p><strong>Plan less, experience more, and leave yourself a reason to return.</strong></p>
`
